{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"f94a19ef9c6e976d","type":"text","text":"Les partis politiques aux Etats-Unis ","x":-380,"y":-40,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cf6cf9cf1c6b8c43","type":"text","text":"- George Washington and the Framers of the Constitution ==viewed== (passé et plus de lien avec le présent) political associations as harmful to the public good. \n->They could ==pit== (toujours au passée et plus de lien au présent) one group of citizens against another. \n\n- Washington ==warned== in his __1796 Farewell Address__ that political parties could divide the nation by replacing the people’s collective will with the interests of a small, ambitious minority. He ==feared== that shifting party victories would make government unstable and driven by conflicting factional goals instead of the nation’s overall good. ","x":-1000,"y":-480,"width":692,"height":240},
		{"id":"3c02bc58dff8c5b3","type":"text","text":"Jefferson's party ==held== (passé) power for 24 years: \n- Jefferson 1800-1808\n- Madison 1808-1816\n- Monroe 1816-1824\nThe Federalist Party ==disappeared== after the War of 1812\n  ","x":480,"y":-440,"width":420,"height":160},
		{"id":"96305eee173abb4a","type":"text","text":"- However, an ideological divide ==was growing== (temps du passé + en même temps de cela il se passait ca (processus long) Be+Ving ). __Federalists, such as Treasury Secretary Hamilton__, opposed __Democratic-Republicans, such as Jefferson and Madison__.\n\n- The Federalists ==wanted== (passé , rupture avec le présent) a federal government with __significant power and authority__ over the states.\nThe Democratic-Republicans, whereas ==preferred== (passé car rupture avec le présent) __limited federal power and greater autonomy__ for the states. ","x":-255,"y":-480,"width":615,"height":240},
		{"id":"c1f72ecdfa604641","type":"text","text":"The __westward expansion__ of the country and __the development of agriculture and slavery in the South__ and __of industry in the North__ ==led== to diverging (2ème verbe en ing) trends","x":125,"y":-166,"width":530,"height":90},
		{"id":"df4892399bca8835","type":"text","text":"- The only remaining party ==splintered== into __the National Republican Party__ of John Quincy Adams and __the Democratic-Republican Party__ \n- The Democratic-Republican Party : \nUnder Jackson , the party's name ==changed== to __Democratic party in 1844__ ; in __1848 ==was created==__ (temps du passé + temps long de création) __the Democratic national Committee__\n\n","x":840,"y":-192,"width":780,"height":142},
		{"id":"2c2f7c0c1b710b99","type":"text","text":"- Cleveland (1884-1892) ==reformed== the civil service system for government employees. \n- The party ==helped== the __new immigrants ==join== American society__. \n- Woodrow Wilson (1912, 1926) ==was== __the first Democratic president of 20^th century__. He ==led== his country into __World War I__ and ==fought== for the __League of Nations__. He also ==created== the __Federal Reserve Board__, __farm-loan__, ==passed== __the first labord and child welfare laws__.\n- In 1932 FD Roosevelt ==was elected== for his promise in his __New Deal__ to lift the country out of the Great Depression. His reforms ==established== the __The Tennesse Valley Authority__ and the __Work Progress Administration__ , ==formed== the __National Industrial Recovery Act__ and ==created== the __Social Security System__. \n-> Blacks who ==had== traditionally ==favored== the Republicans for ending slavery, now ==supported== Democrats for creating unemployment relief. \n- Henry Truman ==fought== discrimination and ==established== the __Marshall Plan__, __Truman Doctrine__ and __NATO__\n- Presidents Kennedy and Johnson ==fought== against segregation with the __Civil Right Act 1964__ and voting __Rights Act 1965__. Johnson ==formed== his __Great Party__, including __Medicare__ in 1965. He ==did== this to fight against poverty. \n- Important moments under Democratic presidents : __the Camp David treaty__ between Israel and Egypt under Carter (1976), and plans for nuclear disposal. Under Clinton, the Democratic-led Congress favored access to higher education with the Student Loan Reform Act and job protection with the Family and Medical Leave Act.","x":1000,"y":-10,"width":1055,"height":380},
		{"id":"3a99a39d0044899a","type":"text","text":"- Around 1850 Alvan Bovay, a Democrat ==disillusioned== by the turmoil caused by __the Kansas-Nebraska Act__, created the __Republican Party__\n- In 1856, John Fremont ==was== the first Republican to run for the White House, but he ==lost==. \n- The first republican president ==was== Lincoln in 1860. During the Civil War, he ==outlawed== __slavery in 1865__, ==established== __equal protection under the law in 1868__, and ==granted== __Blacks voting rights  in 1870__ with the 13th, 14th, and 15th amendments. \n-> While in Power , he ==created== the department of Agriculture and with the __Homestead Act__ he encouraged internal migration by giving 160 acres, if those people ==lived== on the land for 5 years. \n-> He ==was== also a the origine of the ==Morrill Act== of 1862, which ==gave== federal states to build __agricultural and technical college and universities __\n- The 1872 Republican Congress ==authorized== equal pay for equal work for women employed by federal agencies.\n- In 1896, Republican Congress ==proposal== to enfranchise women but it ==was defeated== __4 times by Democratic controlled Senate__. The Equal Suffrage Amendment passed only when the Republicans regained control of Congress. \n- From 1860 to this day, the White House ==has been== in Republican hands __23 times__ and only __14 times__ in the hands of the Democrats.\n\n\n","x":520,"y":565,"width":760,"height":440},
		{"id":"69c5f147c478b972","type":"text","text":"Since its inception, the Republican Party ==has been== the first party to fight for individual rights in opposition to a large government. \nGOP ==stands== for Grand Old Party and refers to __British Prime Minister Gladstone__. ","x":1528,"y":721,"width":679,"height":129},
		{"id":"7bc970c8e95b0f64","type":"text","text":"- Over  50 other parties ==form== the third-party movement, as in 1919 when the Communist Party ==attracted== many workers, but their success ==were== at best local . \n- In 1912, Roosevelt ==ran== under the Progressive party and came second after Democrat Woodrow Wilson. ","x":0,"y":425,"width":480,"height":160},
		{"id":"e5b0a70c3fcb3d37","type":"text","text":"\n- After **Barack Obama’s election in 2008**\n- Many **conservative voters** ==disagreed== with the government spending **billions of tax dollars** on bank bailouts and expensive programs.\n- They ==remembered== the phrase from the **Declaration of Independence** — “**No taxation without representation**.”\n- A person on a web forum mentioned the **Boston Tea Party (December 16, 1773)**, when American colonists protested British taxes.\n- The idea of sending **tea bags** to members of Congress ==inspired== the name of a new conservative movement the **Tea Party**.","x":-308,"y":845,"width":500,"height":320},
		{"id":"9b7677082fa0bf4d","type":"text","text":"- On **February 19, 2009**, CNBC editor **Rick Santelli** ==criticized== __federal plans to refinance mortgages, saying they benefited bad ones__.  \n- He ==launched== the idea of a **“Chicago Tea Party.”** Angry voters created local **Tea Party groups**, and the first **rallies** ==were held== on **February 27** in **Washington** and other cities.\n\n- The **Tea Party Movement** opposed **high taxes**, ==wanted== the **federal government** to reduce its **spending** and **debt**, and presented it self as a **leaderless, grassroots movement**.  \n- Local groups ==used== **social media** such as Facebook, Twitter, YouTube, and blogs to express their discontent ands ideas.  \n- Its main values ==were== **fiscal responsibility**, **limited government**, and **free markets**, ==shared== by groups like **Tea Party Express**, **FreedomWorks**, and the **9/12 Project**.","x":-1240,"y":1120,"width":800,"height":280},
		{"id":"49fed67fcf487a9c","type":"text","text":"- Since **2010**, the **Tea Party** ==has== **no longer ==been a registered== national party**, though the **Boston Tea Party (founded in 2006)** still supports **fiscal conservatism**, **lower taxes**, and **limited government**.\n- In **2010 and 2012**, **Tea Party candidates** ==defeated== moderate Republicans in primaries but later **==lost to Democrats==**, allowing **President Obama** to **keep control of the Senate**. This created **==gridlock==** between the **White House** and **Congress**.\n- In **2013**, **Sarah Palin** ==considered== running for the **Senate (2014)** but instead **==turned== to television**, launching a **reality show** and her **own news channel**.\n- On **June 10, 2014**, the **Tea Party** ==scored== a major win when **David Brat** ==defeated== **Eric Cantor**, the **House majority leader**, in Virginia’s Republican primary. Cantor’s loss ==was linked== to his **support for immigration reform**, seen by conservatives as **“amnesty”** for illegal immigrants.\n- In the **2016 presidential primaries**, **Ted Cruz**, **Rand Paul**, and **Marco Rubio** all ==benefited== from **Tea Party support**. **Donald Trump** also ==praised== Tea Party followers as **“hard-working patriots.”**\n- Although part of the Republican Party, **Tea Party supporters** ==are== **strongly anti-government**, **reject compromise**, and often **criticize GOP leaders** as much as **Democrats**.\n- By **2017**, **support for the Tea Party** ==had declined== among both general voters and Republicans. Yet, many of its **ideas lived on** in the **MAGA (Make America Great Again)** movement within the **Republican Party**.\n- The **donkey** and **elephant** ==became== the traditional **symbols** of the **Democratic** and **Republican** parties. The **donkey**, linked to **Andrew Jackson** in **1828**, ==symbolized== **stubbornness and populism**, while **Thomas Nast**’s **1874 cartoon**==paired== it with the **elephant**. Democrats see the **donkey** as **brave and humble** and the **elephant** as **pompous**, whereas Republicans view the **elephant** as **strong and wise** and the **donkey** as **stubborn and foolish**.","x":-2400,"y":-481,"width":760,"height":701},
		{"id":"0b5ab753c0179cc4","type":"text","text":"- On **October 5, 2011**, **Sarah Palin** ==announced== she **would not ==run== for president** in the **2012 election**. She ==said== she ==wanted== to put **God, family, and country first** and help elect **honest public servants**.\n\n- Palin ==aimed== to **restore America’s greatness**, defend **freedom and free markets**, and promote **energy independence**. She also ==called== for **lower taxes**, **fewer regulations**, and **a smaller government** to support job creation. Her goal ==was== to **replace the President**, **retake the Senate**, and **keep the House** under Republican control.","x":-2840,"y":320,"width":680,"height":213},
		{"id":"6fcaec704ea84002","type":"text","text":"Sarah Palin, born in **1964**, was the **youngest person** and the **first woman** ==elected== **Governor of Alaska**. She ==served== from **2006 to 2009 when she ==resigned==**, after having been a **City Council member** and **mayor of Wasilla** from **1996 to 2002**.\n\nIn **2008**, Republican **John McCain** ==chose== her as his **vice-presidential running mate**, making her the **first Alaskan** and the **first female Republican nominee** for vice president.\n\nShe ==published== her **autobiography in 2009**, followed by _America by Heart_ in **2010**, a book full of **patriotic reflections**. In **February 2010**, during the **Tea Party Convention** in **Nashville**, she ==criticized== President Obama for his weak stance on terrorism and for the country’s growing deficit.\n\nPalin later ==endorsed== Republican candidates for the **2010 midterm elections**, achieving success in **26 out of 37 races**. Her strong influence led many to believe she might ==run== for president in 2012.","x":-1840,"y":845,"width":544,"height":445},
		{"id":"63b22a49be670fa7","type":"text","text":"- In the **2010 midterm elections**, **Tea Party candidates** ==helped== Republicans win **242 House seats**, ending the Democrats’ majority. The **Senate** ==stayed== Democratic with **51 seats**.\n\n- **Rand Paul** (born **1963**), son of **Ron Paul**, ==became== one of the movement’s key senators. He ==ran== for president in 2015, but ==withdrew== after **Donald Trump’s ==rise==**, and was **re-elected to the Senate in 2016**.","x":-2400,"y":640,"width":450,"height":260}
	],
